          <p>
            Two quirks are worth knowing before you compare your own tub. Ryse's panels vary more than most: other listings we saw showed scoops from 30 g to 37.1 g and noticeably different sodium figures, so the 240 mg here is the high end of what we recorded for Jet-Puffed Marshmallow. Ryse also lists only a gum blend without naming the gums, which makes its label harder to judge than Dymatize's.
          </p>

          <p>
            On the Dymatize side, both 5 lb tubs were marked temporarily unavailable at Vitacost when our database was checked, even though prices were displayed. Neither brand prints a fiber figure; all three panels show less than 1 g. Leucine is the one number only Dymatize publishes, at 2.6 g for ISO100 and 2.7 g for Elite per scoop.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Has More Protein per Scoop, Ryse Loaded or ISO100?
          </h2>

          <p>
            They tie at 25 g of protein per scoop, but ISO100 gets there with less powder. Per the labels, ISO100 delivers 25 g in a 32 g scoop, about 78% protein by weight, while Ryse Loaded needs 33.9 g, about 74%. Dymatize's Elite 100% Whey needs a 36 g scoop for the same 25 g, which puts it lowest at about 69%.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 1.9 g difference between Ryse and ISO100 is mostly fat and flavor system. Ryse adds a medium chain triglyceride powder, which is where its 2.5 g of fat comes from, plus inulin, a gum blend and salt. ISO100's extra grams are mainly cocoa, lecithin and salt. That is why Ryse lands at 130 calories and ISO100 at 120 for the same protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ten calories a scoop will not change anyone's results, but the pattern matters to two groups. People tracking macros closely for a cut get a slightly cleaner number from ISO100, with 1 g of fat and 2 g of carbohydrate. People who want the shake to feel more like a milkshake may prefer the extra fat in Ryse, which buyers often credit for its creamier texture.
          </p>

          <p>
            Quality of the protein is close. Both use whey, which is a complete protein rich in leucine, the amino acid most linked to muscle protein synthesis. Dymatize prints 2.6 g of leucine per ISO100 scoop; Ryse does not publish a leucine figure, so we cannot compare them directly. One detail on the Ryse label is easy to miss: the MCT powder contains sodium caseinate, a milk protein, so Ryse is not a whey-only product.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Ryse vs Dymatize: Which Is Cheaper per Gram of Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ryse is cheaper per gram of protein at every price we recorded. At Amazon on 20 November 2026, a 2 lb Ryse Loaded tub was $44.99 for 27 servings, about $1.67 per 25 g, and a 3 lb ISO100 Fruity Pebbles tub was $96.20 for 45 servings, about $2.14 per 25 g. Those Amazon figures were recorded for our <a href='/ryse-vs-ghost-protein/'>Ryse vs Ghost comparison</a> and are reused here so the two pages agree.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Cost Math</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$1.67</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Ryse per 25 g protein (Amazon, 2 lb)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$2.14</p>
