          <p>
            At one 25 g shake a day for 30 days, Ryse costs about $50 and ISO100 about $64 at those Amazon prices, a gap of roughly $14 a month or about $170 a year. ISO100 costs roughly 28% more per gram than Ryse. That is a real premium, but a smaller one than the database figures suggest.
          </p>

          <p>
            The database figures, from Vitacost on 1 November 2026, widen the gap. Ryse was $49.99 for 27 servings, about $1.85 per 25 g, and the 5 lb ISO100 was $196.72 for 71 servings, about $2.77 per 25 g, which would make ISO100 about 50% dearer. We treat that ISO100 price with caution: the tub was marked temporarily unavailable and the figure sits well above the Amazon price, a point also flagged in our <a href='/dymatize-vs-premier-protein/'>Dymatize vs Premier Protein</a> comparison.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tub size moves both numbers. Ryse's 4 lb Chocolate Cookie Blast came to about $1.50 per 25 g in our Ryse vs Ghost check, while Ryse's 2 lb tubs hold only 27 servings, about 675 g of protein. ISO100 is sold up to 5 lb, about 1,775 g of protein, so a sale on the big tub can close much of the gap. To compare any listing, divide the price by the servings, divide by the grams of protein per serving, and multiply by 25.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which Is Third-Party Tested, Ryse or Dymatize?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dymatize is the only one of the two with a sport certification claim: retailer listings for ISO100 and Elite 100% Whey, including the Vitacost pages we used, show the Informed Choice logo. We could not confirm individual flavors in the <a href='https://choice.wetestyoutrust.com/' rel='nofollow noopener' target='_blank'>Informed Choice registry</a>, which returned an error when our database was checked, so treat it as a retailer-listed claim until you look up your exact flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            For Ryse, we found no listing in the <a href='https://www.nsfsport.com/' rel='nofollow noopener' target='_blank'>NSF Certified for Sport</a> registry and no Ryse protein on the Informed Sport or Informed Choice brand lists. Our <a href='/ryse-protein-powder-review/'>Ryse review</a> notes that one retailer mentions unnamed independent lab verification, but that is not a certification you can check yourself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Informed Choice is run by LGC, an anti-doping laboratory, and tests samples of certified products for substances banned in sport, per the program's own description. It is aimed at athletes, and a certification covers the specific products and lots on the register, not a brand as a whole. If you compete under drug testing, that is the deciding factor here, and it points to Dymatize.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are not tested, certification is a useful outside check on what is in the tub, but not the only one. Neither brand has published heavy-metal results in our database, so we cannot say which carries less lead or cadmium. That is a gap in the evidence, not a finding against either powder.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Isolate vs Blend: Which Has the Cleaner Label?
          </h2>

          <p>
            ISO100 has the cleaner label: two forms of whey isolate, cocoa, salt, soy lecithin, flavor, potassium chloride and two sweeteners, with no gums listed. Ryse Loaded has a longer list, including MCT powder with sodium caseinate and silicon dioxide, organic agave inulin, an unnamed gum blend, salt and two artificial sweeteners.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sweeteners split the brands. Ryse pairs sucralose with acesulfame potassium, both artificial. ISO100 Gourmet Chocolate pairs sucralose with steviol glycosides from stevia. Neither is sweetener-free, and taste for sweeteners is personal, but if you are trying to cut back on artificial sweeteners, ISO100 uses one fewer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ryse's extras each have a purpose. MCT powder adds body and a richer mouthfeel, inulin is a prebiotic fiber that also thickens, and the gum blend keeps the shake from separating. The trade-off is that inulin and gums are the ingredients most often blamed for gas in sensitive drinkers, and Ryse does not say which gums it uses, which our scoring treats as a transparency gap.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sodium is the one place Ryse looks worse on paper by a clear margin, at 240 mg per scoop against 180 mg for ISO100, per the panels we read. Both list milk and soy as allergens in our records, so neither suits a soy-free diet. Elite 100% Whey, for comparison, carries 160 mg of sodium but adds carrageenan and two other gums.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Tastes Better and Mixes Easier?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ryse is usually described by buyers as the richer, more dessert-like shake, while ISO100 is described as thinner and cleaner. We have not tasted either ourselves; this section reflects published buyer reviews and the flavor lists on retailer and brand pages.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both brands sell on licensed flavors. Ryse's list includes Jet-Puffed Marshmallow, Skippy Peanut Butter, Little Debbie Cosmic Brownies, Blueberry Muffin and Chocolate Cookie Blast, and the brand lists about 30 options in total. ISO100's range includes Fruity Pebbles, Cocoa Pebbles, Birthday Cake Pebbles and two Dunkin' flavors alongside Gourmet Chocolate, Gourmet Vanilla and Fudge Brownie.
          </p>

          <p>
            Texture follows the label. Ryse's MCT powder, inulin and gums give a thicker body that many buyers like in milk; some find it heavy in water. ISO100 is an isolate with lecithin and no gums, and buyers commonly report it mixing in a shaker with water without clumps, with a lighter, less creamy result.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you drink your shake with water, ISO100's cleaner, thinner style tends to suit it better. If you mix with milk or want a dessert substitute, Ryse is built for that. The cheapest way to find out is the smallest tub in the flavor you are most curious about.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stomach" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Is Easier on a Lactose-Sensitive Stomach?
          </h2>

          <p>
            ISO100 is the safer choice for a lactose-sensitive stomach, because it uses only whey isolate and hydrolyzed whey isolate. Isolate is filtered to remove most of the lactose and fat, and the hydrolyzed portion is partly pre-digested. Ryse Loaded contains whey concentrate, which keeps more lactose, plus sodium caseinate and inulin.
          </p>

          <p>
            Neither brand publishes a lactose figure, so we cannot give milligrams. What the labels do show is direction: ISO100's 2 g of carbohydrate per scoop sets an upper limit on how much milk sugar it can carry, and Ryse's 3 g includes some inulin and flavoring as well. Most people with mild lactose intolerance tolerate small amounts, so the difference matters more for some than others.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inulin is the other suspect for gas or bloating. It is a fermentable fiber that some people react to, and Ryse does not print how much a scoop contains, so we cannot say whether the amount is meaningful. If Ryse bothers you and ISO100 does not, the concentrate and the inulin are the likely reasons. If ISO100 bothers you too, consider a plant protein; our <a href='/best-whey-protein-powder/'>best whey protein powder guide</a> lists other isolates first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="elite" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Is Dymatize Elite 100% Whey a Cheaper Way to Get Dymatize?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not at the prices we recorded: Elite 100% Whey cost about $2.65 per 25 g of protein at Vitacost, more than Ryse at either retailer and only a little less than ISO100's Vitacost price. At $166.95 for about 63 servings of the 5 lb Rich Chocolate tub, Elite was not the budget Dymatize option on 1 November 2026.
          </p>

          <p>
            That cuts against its usual reputation. Our <a href='/dymatize-protein-powder-review/'>Dymatize review</a> notes that Elite typically runs 20 to 30 percent cheaper per gram than ISO100, and on sale it often does. But a blend built on concentrate, isolate and hydrolyzed concentrate, with a 36 g scoop, 140 calories and 3 g of fat, is closer to Ryse in style than to ISO100, and it scores 48 out of 100 against Ryse's 56.
          </p>

          <p>
            Elite does keep one Dymatize advantage over Ryse: retailer listings show the Informed Choice logo, and Dymatize prints 2.7 g of leucine per scoop. If you are drug tested and want a cheaper blend than ISO100, Elite on sale is the route to take. If you are not, Ryse gives the same 25 g in fewer calories for less money, at the prices we have.
          </p>

          <p>
            We confirmed only the Rich Chocolate flavor for Elite in our database, and did not record an Amazon price for it. Check the cost per 25 g of the tub in front of you before assuming either Dymatize powder costs more or less than Ryse.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ryse-pros-cons" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. What Ryse Loaded Protein Does Well and Where It Falls Short
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ryse Loaded does price and flavor well, and falls short on transparency and certification. Its strongest points are the lowest cost per gram of the three powders here, at about $1.67 per 25 g on Amazon and about $1.50 in the 4 lb tub per our earlier check, and a licensed flavor list that few rivals match, from Skippy to Little Debbie.
          </p>

          <p>
            Its weaker points are all on the label. It is a whey blend with concentrate rather than an isolate, it uses two artificial sweeteners, it lists an unnamed gum blend, and the Jet-Puffed panel carries 240 mg of sodium. We found no sport certification. Panels also vary a lot by flavor, so the numbers on your tub may differ from ours. That adds up to a Label Score of 56 out of 100.
          </p>

          <p>
            For Ryse's other lines, including Loaded Plant Protein and its clear whey, see our <a href='/ryse-protein-powder-review/'>full Ryse protein powder review</a>. For how Ryse fares against another flavor-led brand, our <a href='/ryse-vs-ghost-protein/'>Ryse vs Ghost Protein</a> comparison covers enzymes and sweeteners.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="iso-pros-cons" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            13. What Dymatize ISO100 Does Well and Where It Falls Short
          </h2>

          <p>
            ISO100 does purity and digestibility well, and falls short on price. It packs 25 g of protein into 32 g of powder at 120 calories, 1 g of fat and 1 g of sugar, uses only whey isolate, prints 2.6 g of leucine, carries the Informed Choice logo on retailer listings and avoids gums. Those points earn it 64 out of 100, the highest score in this comparison.
          </p>

          <p>
            Its weak points start with cost: about $2.14 per 25 g at the Amazon price we saw and about $2.77 at Vitacost. It still uses sucralose, lists soy from lecithin, and its certification could not be confirmed in the registry on the day we checked. Our label data came from a retailer, not Dymatize, because we could not find the product page on Dymatize's site.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our <a href='/dymatize-protein-powder-review/'>Dymatize protein powder review</a> covers Elite Casein and Super Mass Gainer as well. For a look at ISO100 against a grocery-aisle brand, see <a href='/dymatize-vs-premier-protein/'>Dymatize vs Premier Protein</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

