          </p>

          <p>
            If neither brand fits, the <a href='/best-whey-protein-powder/'>best whey protein powder</a> rankings sort every whey we track by Label Score and cost per 25 g, and our <a href='/protein-powder-comparison/'>protein powder comparison</a> tool lets you filter by certification or protein type.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip-both" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            15. Who Should Skip Both?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip both if you cannot have dairy or soy, eat vegan, or want a powder without sweeteners. Ryse, ISO100 and Elite are all milk-based, all three list soy in our records, and every flavor we checked uses sucralose with a second sweetener.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip both if you want an unflavored whey for cooking or mixing into food. Both brands are built around flavored tubs, and the licensed flavors are a large part of what you pay for. Plain, unflavored isolate from a bulk brand will usually cost less per gram and leave you in charge of the taste.
          </p>

          <p>
            Also skip both if your only goal is the lowest cost per gram. Big-tub store brands and mainstream wheys often come in below Ryse's $1.67 per 25 g, and some carry certifications too. Our <a href='/protein-powder-comparison/'>comparison database</a> lets you sort by cost per 25 g to find them.
          </p>
        </div>

