        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            17. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading 25 g on both tubs as a tie</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Both labels say 25 g of protein, but ISO100 delivers it in 32 g of powder and Ryse needs 33.9 g, with 2.5 g of fat against 1 g. On a strict cut those extra calories and fat add up over months. Compare protein by weight and fat per scoop, not just the headline number.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Calling Ryse an isolate because isolate is listed first</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ryse lists instantized whey isolate first, but the blend also contains whey concentrate and sodium caseinate in its MCT powder. If you chose it because concentrate upsets you, the label does not support that. ISO100 is the isolate-only option of the two brands; Ryse is a blend.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Vitacost's ISO100 price with Amazon's Ryse price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Mixing retailers distorts the gap. Vitacost's 5 lb ISO100 worked out to about $2.77 per 25 g while Amazon's 3 lb was about $2.14, and Ryse ran $1.85 at Vitacost against $1.67 on Amazon. Compare both tubs at the same store on the same day before deciding the premium is too high.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Elite 100% Whey is always the cheap Dymatize</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Elite is usually cheaper than ISO100, but at Vitacost's list price it came to about $2.65 per 25 g, more than Ryse and close to ISO100. Brand habits are not prices. Work out cost per 25 g on the day you buy, and wait for a sale if Elite is your pick.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a certification logo without checking the flavor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The Informed Choice logo appears on ISO100 retailer listings, but certification covers named products and lots, and we could not confirm flavors in the registry. Drug-tested athletes should look up the exact flavor and size on the Informed Choice site. Ryse has no listing to check at all.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            18. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Ryse or Dymatize better?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It depends on what you need. Dymatize ISO100 is the better-built powder: 25 g of protein in a 32 g scoop, 1 g of fat, isolate only and an Informed Choice logo on retailer listings. Ryse Loaded is cheaper at about $1.67 per 25 g against about $2.14 on Amazon and offers more dessert-style flavors. Tested or lactose-sensitive buyers should pick ISO100; everyone else can save with Ryse.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Dymatize ISO100 vs Ryse Loaded Protein a fair comparison?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, because they are each brand's flagship and both give 25 g of protein per scoop. The difference is type: ISO100 is hydrolyzed whey isolate, while Ryse Loaded blends whey isolate, whey concentrate and MCT powder. That explains why ISO100 has less fat and lactose and Ryse a creamier body, and why ISO100 costs more per gram of protein.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Ryse protein third-party tested like Dymatize?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not that we could verify. We found no Ryse protein in the NSF Certified for Sport registry or on Informed Choice and Informed Sport brand lists. Dymatize ISO100 and Elite 100% Whey show the Informed Choice logo on retailer listings, though the registry returned an error when we checked, so confirm your exact flavor before a drug-tested event.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which has fewer calories, Ryse or Dymatize?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Dymatize ISO100 has fewer: 120 calories per 32 g scoop against 130 for Ryse Loaded's 33.9 g scoop, per the labels we read, both with 25 g of protein. Fat is the main reason, at 1 g for ISO100 and 2.5 g for Ryse. Dymatize Elite 100% Whey is the highest of the three, at 140 calories and 3 g of fat.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Ryse Loaded Protein contain casein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, a small amount. The MCT powder in Ryse Loaded is listed with sodium caseinate, a milk protein, alongside the whey isolate and concentrate. Ryse does not say how much. It matters mainly if you avoid casein specifically or assumed Ryse was whey only. Dymatize ISO100's label lists whey isolate and hydrolyzed whey isolate with no casein.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Dymatize ISO100 worth the extra money over Ryse?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It is if you are drug tested, sensitive to lactose or tracking macros tightly. At the Amazon prices we recorded it costs about 28% more per gram than Ryse, roughly $14 a month at one shake a day. For an untested lifter who digests whey concentrate comfortably, that premium buys a leaner label rather than better results.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which mixes better, Ryse or Dymatize ISO100?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Buyers commonly report both mixing easily in a shaker bottle. ISO100 is an isolate with lecithin and no gums, so it tends to come out thin and smooth in water. Ryse's MCT powder, inulin and gum blend make it thicker and creamier, which buyers like in milk. We have not mixed either ourselves; these are buyer reports.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

